preConditionHook:
// The bar traded down to or through the moving average and closed back above
// it (long) -- the countertrend push that traps with-trend traders out and
// fails. Mirrored on the short side. Unlike EmaTouch this reads where the
// bar CLOSED, so it is directional.
//
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_Xaverage, ported from EmaGapBar (the EMA reference,
// which carries the full measurements). In short, from
// EL_XAverage_Probe.txt (@OJ 240min, chart starts 2007 and 2010):
//   * the wrapper is BIT-IDENTICAL to the plain XAverage built-in at a fixed
//     length -- (wf - xa) * 1e12 printed exactly 0.0000 on all 17,785 bars;
//   * the SEED is the PRICE on the study's first calculated bar
//     (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1), not a length-term simple average, and an EMA
//     never forgets its seed -- it only decays at (1 - sf)^n, ~330 bars;
//   * the recurrence is x + sf*(p - x), ONE statement, not sf*p + (1-sf)*x:
//     the two separated on 67% of probed bars past 100;
//   * there is NO warm-up hole -- do not add a bar-count guard returning 0;
//   * UNMEASURED: a walkforward length change, so emaLength is PINNED in the
//     grid; and the lagged read XAverage(...)[k], which this rule does not
//     need because it reads the current value only.
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration
// is a redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy.
// Every one is fully assigned each bar before it is read.
emaSf = 2.0 / (emaLength + 1.0);
if (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1) {
    emaVal = close[0];
} else {
    emaVal = emaVal + emaSf * (close[0] - emaVal);
}
